  "body": "What happens: three byte-pin tests fail on a Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true`:\n\n- `example_catalog`: `every_catalog_case_opens_regenerates_verifies_and_posts_byte_for_byte`, `every_catalog_case_rebuilds_from_camscript` - the catalog case `2d/first-part`.\n- `first_part_programs`: `first_part_posts_the_shipped_program`, `first_router_part_posts_the_shipped_program`.\n\nThe tests compare the posted program with the shipped `.nc` file byte for byte. The post writes LF line ends. `.gitattributes` pins the goldens (`crates/app/tests/golden/**`) to LF, and it pins `examples/first-4axis-part.nc`. It does not pin the other shipped example programs. With `core.autocrlf=true`, git checks those files out with CRLF. `git ls-files --eol` shows `i/lf w/crlf` for each of them:\n\n- `examples/first-part.nc`\n- `examples/first-router-part.nc`\n- `examples/catalog/2d/first-part/first-part.nc`\n\nSo the comparison fails on Windows only. The post-merge gate on Linux passes them. I saw this on the quick gate of PR #2352, 2026-11-18.\n\nExpected: the byte-pin suites give the same result in every contributor's checkout.\n\nFix: add the missing pins to `.gitattributes`, one rule for the example programs and one for the catalog programs, both `text eol=lf`. Check first whether the test of `examples/first-4axis-part.nc` and the site tests read other shipped `.nc` files without a pin.\n\nDone means: the three tests pass in a fresh Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true`, and `git ls-files --eol examples/` shows `w/lf` for every `.nc` file.\n",
